tel 1.0

base embarcadero
import soundness

info   OCI container images and Wasm artifacts as values, and a typed gRPC client for containerd
detail
    _Embarcadero_ covers the two halves of running software in containers. The first is the
    **image**: a stack of filesystem layers and a configuration, each piece addressed by the
    digest of its bytes, in the format the Open Container Initiative (OCI) standardises and
    Docker, containerd, podman and every registry understand. The second is the **daemon**:
    containerd, the runtime beneath Docker and Kubernetes, driven over gRPC on a local socket
    to register containers, run tasks inside them, and manage images and namespaces.

    An `Image` is assembled in memory from `Layer`s (each a `Tarfile`, gzip-compressed and
    hashed twice, once uncompressed for its `diffId` and once as stored for its `digest`) and
    an `Image.Config` whose `ContainerConfig` says what to run. The library computes the
    config blob, the `Oci.Manifest` which ties config and layers together by digest, and the
    single-entry `Index`; `Image#archive` renders the whole thing as an OCI image-layout tar
    (an "oci-archive") which `docker load`, `ctr images import`, `podman load` or `skopeo` will
    accept. `Image.wasm` builds the same layout as a Wasm OCI Artifact, whose one layer is an
    `application/wasm` component and whose config is a `WasmConfig` naming the WASI
    generation and the Component Model interfaces the component imports and exports. An
    existing archive is read by opening it, `data.open[Image]()` or `path.open[Image]()`,
    which gives an `Image.Handle` for the scope of a block: its `index`, `manifest`, `config`
    and each `layer`, verified against the digests the manifest declares, with an `Oci.Error`
    for anything missing, corrupt or unsupported.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    def entry(name: Text, content: Text): Tar.Entry =
      Tar.Entry.File
        ( path  = name.as[Relative on Tar],
          mode  = UnixMode(),
          user  = UnixUser(0),
          group = UnixGroup(0),
          mtime = 0.bits.u32,
          data  = Archive.Body(content.in[Data]) )

    val layer: Layer = Layer(Tarfile(List(entry(t"hello.txt", t"hello world\n"))))
    val image: Image = Image(List(layer), config = ContainerConfig(Cmd = List(t"/bin/sh")))

    image.manifest.layers.map(_.digest)   // List(layer.digest)
    val archive: Data = image.archive.source[Data].memoize

    archive.open[Image](): handle ?=>
      handle.imageConfig.rootfs.diff_ids  // List(layer.diffId)
    ```

    A `Containerd` client connects to a daemon through an `Http2.Endpoint` (normally a Unix
    socket carrying cleartext HTTP/2) inside a `supervise` scope, bound to one containerd
    namespace, and each of its methods is one gRPC call exchanging typed records: a
    `Containerd.Container` is registered with `createContainer`, given a root filesystem of `Containerd.Mount`s and
    a task with `createTask`, started, signalled, awaited and deleted with the `…Task`
    methods, and inspected as a `Workload` whose `state` is a `Containerd.ProcessStatus`. The
    `Workload` openable binds that whole lifecycle to a scope,
    `container.open[Workload](Read & Launch & Signalling)`, so a task is created, optionally
    started, and always killed, reaped and deleted when the block ends. The messages on the
    wire — every `…Request` and `…Response`, `Containerd.AnyMessage`, `Containerd.Timestamp`, `Containerd.Empty` — are
    ordinary case classes with protobuf field numbers, so they round-trip with the daemon and
    can be encoded and decoded directly through locomotion's `Protobuf`.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import logging.silentLogging
    import probates.awaitProbate
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import threads.virtualThreads

    def report[endpoint](endpoint: Http2.Endpoint[endpoint]): Unit = supervise:
      val client = Containerd(endpoint, t"default")
      client.version().version
      client.containers().map(_.id)
    ```

    The library has three source sets:

    - `embarcadero.oci` — `Image`, `Layer`, `Oci`, `Descriptor`, `Index`, `RootFs`,
      `History`, `ContainerConfig`, `WasmConfig` and `WasmComponent`, and
      `Image.dataOpenable` for opening in-memory `Data` as an image. It compiles for the
      JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native, so an image can be assembled in a browser or a Wasm
      guest; it draws on bitumen for its tar layers, pneumatic for gzip, gastronomy for
      SHA-256 and jacinta for the JSON documents.
    - `embarcadero.oci` on the JVM additionally has `ImageOpenable` and the given
      `imagePathOpenable`, which open a filesystem *path* as an image through bitumen's
      disk-backed tar reader.
    - `embarcadero.containerd` — JVM only: `Containerd`, `Workload`, the `Launch` and
      `Signalling` modes, `Containerd.ProcessStatus`, `Containerd.Event`, and the protobuf message types,
      over obligatory's gRPC and telekinesis's HTTP/2.

    The containerd protobuf records — `Containerd.Container`, `Containerd.Mount`,
    `Containerd.Timestamp` and the service requests and replies — are nested in `Containerd`, so
    they do not compete with other libraries' names in the `soundness` umbrella. The
    givens the library itself needs (`providers.javaBaseProvider`, `alphabets.hexLowerCase`,
    `formatting.compactJsonFormatting`, `codepages.utf8Codepage`) are imported inside it, so
    a program imports only what its own code uses; the openables (`Image.dataOpenable`,
    `Workload.openable`) sit in their companions and resolve with no import, while
    `imagePathOpenable` is a top-level given reached through `import soundness.*`. A
    containerd call needs a `Monitor` (from `supervise`) and tactics for `Grpc.Error`,
    `Http2.Error`, `Async.Error` and `Protobuf.Error`, and logs a `Containerd.Event` for each
    change it makes, so a `Containerd.Event is Loggable` such as `logging.silentLogging` must be
    in scope.

refer embarcadero.Image
refer embarcadero.Layer
refer embarcadero.Oci
refer embarcadero.Containerd
refer embarcadero.Workload

entry .Image   an OCI image or Wasm artifact assembled in memory: layers, config, manifest and index
  detail
      An `Image` is the complete, digest-consistent content of an OCI image: its `#layers`,
      the `#config` document and its rendered `#configBytes`, the `#manifest` which names
      the config and each layer by `Descriptor`, and the single-entry `#index` which names
      the manifest. All of it is computed when the image is built, so the digests and sizes
      are right by construction, and `#archive` renders it as the OCI image-layout tar a
      runtime imports. The companion builds an image two ways: `.apply` from filesystem
      layers and a `ContainerConfig`, and `.wasm` from a compiled Wasm component. Reading an
      image back is a scoped *open*: `.Handle` is what `data.open[Image]()` (through
      `.dataOpenable`) or `path.open[Image]()` (through `imagePathOpenable`, JVM only)
      provides for the duration of a block.

      The pieces come together in a fixed order, each addressed by the digest of the one
      before:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A layer: a tar, compressed and hashed twice
        code
            import soundness.*
            import codepages.utf8Codepage
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            def entry(name: Text, content: Text): Tar.Entry =
              Tar.Entry.File
                ( path  = name.as[Relative on Tar],
                  mode  = UnixMode(),
                  user  = UnixUser(0),
                  group = UnixGroup(0),
                  mtime = 0.bits.u32,
                  data  = Archive.Body(content.in[Data]) )

            val layer: Layer = Layer(Tarfile(List(entry(t"hello.txt", t"hello world\n"))))
            layer.diffId   // sha256:… of the uncompressed tar
            layer.digest   // sha256:… of the gzipped blob, as stored
      frame
        label The config: platform, rootfs diff_ids and what to run
        code
            import soundness.*
            import codepages.utf8Codepage
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            def entry(name: Text, content: Text): Tar.Entry =
              Tar.Entry.File
                ( path  = name.as[Relative on Tar],
                  mode  = UnixMode(),
                  user  = UnixUser(0),
                  group = UnixGroup(0),
                  mtime = 0.bits.u32,
                  data  = Archive.Body(content.in[Data]) )

            val layer: Layer = Layer(Tarfile(List(entry(t"hello.txt", t"hello world\n"))))
            val image: Image = Image(List(layer), config = ContainerConfig(Cmd = List(t"/bin/sh")))
            image.imageConfig.let(_.rootfs.diff_ids)   // List(layer.diffId)
            image.configDescriptor.digest              // sha256:… of the config JSON
      frame
        label The manifest: the config and each layer, by descriptor
        code
            import soundness.*
            import codepages.utf8Codepage
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            def entry(name: Text, content: Text): Tar.Entry =
              Tar.Entry.File
                ( path  = name.as[Relative on Tar],
                  mode  = UnixMode(),
                  user  = UnixUser(0),
                  group = UnixGroup(0),
                  mtime = 0.bits.u32,
                  data  = Archive.Body(content.in[Data]) )

            val layer: Layer = Layer(Tarfile(List(entry(t"hello.txt", t"hello world\n"))))
            val image: Image = Image(List(layer), config = ContainerConfig(Cmd = List(t"/bin/sh")))
            image.manifest.config.digest            // the config descriptor's digest
            image.manifest.layers.map(_.digest)     // List(layer.digest)
            image.manifestDescriptor.digest         // sha256:… of the manifest JSON
      frame
        label The index and the archive: one manifest, written as an oci-layout tar
        code
            import soundness.*
            import codepages.utf8Codepage
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            def entry(name: Text, content: Text): Tar.Entry =
              Tar.Entry.File
                ( path  = name.as[Relative on Tar],
                  mode  = UnixMode(),
                  user  = UnixUser(0),
                  group = UnixGroup(0),
                  mtime = 0.bits.u32,
                  data  = Archive.Body(content.in[Data]) )

            val layer: Layer = Layer(Tarfile(List(entry(t"hello.txt", t"hello world\n"))))
            val image: Image = Image(List(layer), config = ContainerConfig(Cmd = List(t"/bin/sh")))
            image.index.manifests.map(_.digest)     // List(image.manifestDescriptor.digest)
            val archive: Data = image.archive.source[Data].memoize   // oci-layout, index.json, blobs/sha256/…
      ##
      ```

      An `Image` is a plain case class with no capability of its own, so it can be built
      anywhere — on the JVM, in Scala.js or on Scala Native — and handed to code which
      writes the archive to a file, pushes it to a registry, or imports it into a daemon.
  refer embarcadero.Layer
  refer embarcadero.Oci
  refer embarcadero.ContainerConfig
  refer embarcadero.WasmConfig
  entry .apply   assembles an image from filesystem layers and an optional runtime configuration
      `Image(layers, config, architecture, os, annotations)` builds the `Image.Config`
      (`architecture` defaults to `amd64` and `os` to `linux`; `rootfs` is the layers'
      `diffId`s in order; `config` is the `ContainerConfig`, if any), renders it as compact
      JSON for its config blob, descriptors it with the media type
      `application/vnd.oci.image.config.v1+json`, wraps it and the layers' descriptors in
      an `Oci.Manifest` (carrying `annotations`, if any), and names that manifest from a
      single-entry `Index`. Nothing is raised: every digest is computed from bytes already
      in memory.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val layer: Layer = Layer(Tarfile(Nil))

      val image: Image =
        Image
          ( List(layer),
            config       = ContainerConfig(Cmd = List(t"/bin/sh"), WorkingDir = t"/app"),
            architecture = t"arm64",
            annotations  = Map(t"org.opencontainers.image.title" -> t"hello") )

      image.imageConfig.let(_.architecture)   // t"arm64"
      ```
  entry .wasm   assembles a Wasm OCI Artifact: one uncompressed `application/wasm` layer under a Wasm config
      `Image.wasm(component, exports, imports, target, architecture, os, annotations)` is the
      form the Wasm OCI Artifact layout prescribes for a WebAssembly workload. The one layer is
      `Layer.blob(component, media"application/wasm")`: stored verbatim, so its `diffId` and
      `digest` coincide. The config blob is a `WasmConfig` (media type
      `application/vnd.wasm.config.v0+json`) whose `architecture` is `wasm` and whose `os`
      names the WASI generation (`wasip2` by default), with `layerDigests` naming the
      component blob and `component` recording the `exports`, `imports` and optional `target`
      world as a `WasmComponent`. The manifest, index and archive are the ordinary OCI ones, so
      the same registries and tools carry it; a runtime dispatches on the two media types to
      hand the component to a Wasm engine (through a shim such as `io.containerd.wasmtime.v1`)
      rather than a rootfs to `runc`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val component: Data = hex"0061736d01000000"   // a compiled component's bytes

      val artifact: Image =
        Image.wasm
          ( component,
            exports = List(t"wasi:http/incoming-handler@0.2.0"),
            imports = List(t"wasi:io/streams@0.2.0"),
            target  = t"wasi:http/proxy@0.2.0" )

      artifact.wasmConfig.let(_.os)                   // t"wasip2"
      artifact.manifest.layers.map(_.mediaType)       // List(media"application/wasm")
      ```

      The interfaces are usually not typed by hand but read from the WIT world the component
      was linked against: xenophile's `WitDialect.worlds(wit)(name)` gives a world whose
      `exports` and `imports` pass straight through, which is how anthology's OCI edge and
      the repository's own Wasm end-to-end packager (`tests/wasm/packager`) build the
      artifacts they run under wasmtime and `wasmtime serve`. The bytes are never parsed: an
      arbitrary payload is packaged just as a real component is.
  entry .dataOpenable   the given behind `data.open[Image]()`: opens in-memory bytes as an OCI archive
      A given in the companion, so it resolves with no import. It provides an
      `Image.DataOpenable` and needs a `Tactic[Oci.Error]`, a `Tactic[Tar.Error]` and a
      `Tactic[Truncation.Error]` in scope, which it captures. Opening a filesystem *path*
      is `imagePathOpenable`, in the JVM source set.
  entry .Config   an OCI image config document: platform, rootfs diff_id chain, run configuration and history
    detail
        The config blob of a filesystem image (media type
        `application/vnd.oci.image.config.v1+json`), as `Image.apply` writes it and
        `Image.Handle#imageConfig` reads it back. It records the target `#architecture` and
        `#os`, the `#rootfs` whose `diff_ids` are the uncompressed layers' digests in order,
        the optional `#config` (a `ContainerConfig` of what to run), the build `#history`
        and the `#created` time and `#author`. Its JSON encoding and decoding derive
        through jacinta, and the member names are the wire names, so `created` renders as
        `created`. Together with `WasmConfig` it makes up `Oci.Config`, the union a reader
        gets from `Image.Handle#config` when it does not know which kind of artifact it
        opened.
    entry #architecture   the CPU architecture the image targets, such as `amd64` or `arm64`
    entry #os   the operating system the image targets, such as `linux`
    entry #rootfs   the `RootFs`: the ordered `diff_id`s of the uncompressed layers
    entry #config   the `ContainerConfig` saying how to run the image, or `Unset`
    entry #history   how each layer came to be, one `History` per layer or empty change, or `Unset`
    entry #created   when the image was built, as RFC 3339 text, or `Unset`
    entry #author   who built the image, or `Unset`
  entry .Handle   the scoped view of an opened OCI archive: its index, manifest, config and blobs
    detail
        The capability `data.open[Image]()` or `path.open[Image]()` provides for the
        duration of its block, and only then: it is an exclusive capability, so it cannot be
        stored or returned. The archive's entries are read once and held for the handle's
        lifetime, indexed by name, so every lookup after the first is a map access; archives
        `Image#archive` writes put `index.json` before the blobs, so the metadata path is
        cheap. Every method takes a `Tactic[Oci.Error]` and raises `Oci.Error` for a missing
        or unreadable document or blob.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import codepages.utf8Codepage
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val image: Image = Image(List(Layer(Tarfile(Nil))))
        val archive: Data = image.archive.source[Data].memoize

        archive.open[Image](): handle ?=>
          handle.index                                          // the top-level index
          handle.manifest                                       // the manifest it names, digest-checked
          handle.imageConfig                                    // the image config
          handle.manifest.layers.head.let(handle.verified(_))   // a layer's blob, digest-checked
        ```

        A layer is reached three ways, which makes the cost explicit: `#compressed` streams
        the stored bytes untouched, `#layer` decompresses them as a stream, and `#verified`
        gathers them and checks digest and size against the descriptor. A reader which does
        not know whether it has a filesystem image or a Wasm artifact asks for `#config`,
        which dispatches on the config descriptor's media type.

        Opening for writing is refused: `archive.open[Image](Write)` raises `Oci.Error` with
        `WriteUnsupported`.
    refer embarcadero.Oci
    entry #blob   a blob by its `sha256:…` digest, as a stream of its stored chunks, unverified
        The raw stored bytes (for a layer, the gzip-compressed tar) under
        `blobs/sha256/<hex>`, as a `Stream[Data] over Credit`. Raises `Oci.Error` with
        `UnsupportedDigest` for a digest whose algorithm is not `sha256`, and `MissingBlob`
        when the archive has no such blob. Nothing is checked: `#verified` is the checked
        read.
    entry #index   the decoded top-level `index.json`, after checking the `oci-layout` marker
        Reads the `oci-layout` document and requires its `imageLayoutVersion` to begin with
        `1.`, then decodes `index.json` as an `Index`. Raises `Oci.Error` with
        `MissingLayout`, `UnsupportedLayout`, `MissingIndex` or, for a document which does
        not parse, `InvalidBlob`.
    entry #manifest   the index's first manifest, or the one a descriptor selects, digest-verified
        `handle.manifest` takes the first descriptor of `#index` (raising `Oci.Error` with
        `NoManifest` if there is none); `handle.manifest(descriptor)` takes a chosen one. The
        manifest blob is read through `#verified`, so a corrupted manifest is reported as
        `DigestMismatch` rather than decoded, and then decoded as an `Oci.Manifest`.
    entry #imageConfig   the decoded `Image.Config` of a manifest, by default the first
        `handle.imageConfig` or `handle.imageConfig(manifest)`: the manifest's config blob,
        verified and decoded as an `Image.Config`. For a Wasm artifact the blob is a
        `WasmConfig`, and decoding it here fails with `InvalidBlob`; use `#config` when the
        kind is not known.
    entry #wasmConfig   the decoded `WasmConfig` of a manifest, by default the first
        `handle.wasmConfig` or `handle.wasmConfig(manifest)`: the manifest's config blob,
        verified and decoded as a `WasmConfig`.
    entry #config   the config blob in whichever form its media type says: `Image.Config` or `WasmConfig`
        `handle.config` or `handle.config(manifest)` returns an `Oci.Config`, decoding the
        blob as a `WasmConfig` when the config descriptor's media type is
        `application/vnd.wasm.config.v0+json` and as an `Image.Config` otherwise. This is the
        entry point for a runtime deciding whether to unpack a rootfs or instantiate a
        component:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val archive: Data = Image.wasm(hex"0061736d01000000").archive.source[Data].memoize

        archive.open[Image](): handle ?=>
          handle.config match
            case config: WasmConfig   => config.component.let(_.exports)
            case config: Image.Config => config.rootfs.diff_ids
        ```
    entry #compressed   a layer's stored blob, verbatim: for an OCI layer, the gzip-compressed tar
        `#blob` by the descriptor's digest, as a `Stream[Data] over Credit`; the cheapest
        way to copy a layer from one archive to another.
    entry #layer   a layer's content as the uncompressed tar byte stream
        Streams `#compressed` through a gzip decoder when the descriptor's media type carries
        the `+gzip` suffix, and verbatim otherwise — so a Wasm artifact's
        `application/wasm` layer comes back as the component's own bytes. The stream is not
        checked against the digest, since checking would mean draining it first.
    entry #verified   a blob gathered eagerly and checked against its descriptor's digest and size
        Reads the whole blob into a `Data`, computes its SHA-256 and compares it with the
        descriptor's `digest`, raising `Oci.Error` with `DigestMismatch(expected, actual)` if
        they differ, and compares its length with the descriptor's `size`, raising
        `InvalidBlob` if they differ. The opt-in checked path, used by `#manifest`,
        `#imageConfig` and `#wasmConfig` for the documents they decode.
  entry .DataOpenable   the `Openable` which opens in-memory `Data` as an OCI archive, read-only
    detail
        The class `Image.dataOpenable` provides, named rather than anonymous so that its
        result type is stated exactly. It opens a `Data` (`Self`) as an `Image` (`Form`)
        with no flags (`Operand = Nothing`), giving an `Image.Handle` (`Result`) for the
        block. It needs a `Tactic[Oci.Error]`, a `Tactic[Tar.Error]` and a
        `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`. A mode including `Write` is refused with `Oci.Error`
        (`WriteUnsupported`): archives are read, and written only by `Image#archive`.
    entry #Self   `Data`: what is opened
    entry #Form   `Image`: the form it is opened as
    entry #Operand   `Nothing`: there are no open flags
    entry #Result   `Image.Handle`: what the block receives
    entry #open   reads the bytes as a tar, builds an `Image.Handle` over its entries and runs the block
  entry #layers   the image's layers, in order, bottom-most first
  entry #config   the config document: an `Image.Config` for a filesystem image, a `WasmConfig` for a Wasm artifact
      The `Oci.Config` union; `#imageConfig` and `#wasmConfig` narrow it without a match.
  entry #configBytes   the config blob as rendered: compact JSON, the bytes the config digest is taken over
  entry #configDescriptor   the descriptor of the config blob, as the manifest references it
  entry #manifest   the `Oci.Manifest` naming the config and each layer by descriptor
  entry #manifestBytes   the manifest blob as rendered: compact JSON
  entry #manifestDescriptor   the descriptor of the manifest blob, as the index references it
  entry #index   the single-entry `Index` naming the manifest
  entry #indexBytes   the `index.json` document as rendered: compact JSON
  entry #imageConfig   the config as an `Image.Config`, or `Unset` for a Wasm artifact
  entry #wasmConfig   the config as a `WasmConfig`, or `Unset` for a filesystem image
  entry #blobs   every blob as a `(digest, bytes)` pair: the config, each layer, then the manifest
      The content `#archive` writes under `blobs/sha256/`, keyed by the canonical
      `sha256:<hex>` digest of each.
  entry #archive   the image as an OCI image-layout tar: `oci-layout`, `index.json` and `blobs/sha256/…`
      A `Tarfile` holding the `oci-layout` marker (`{"imageLayoutVersion":"1.0.0"}`), the
      `index.json`, and one file per entry of `#blobs` named by its hex digest, each with mode
      `UnixMode()`, owner and group `0` and mtime `0`, so the archive is reproducible. Read it
      as bytes with `image.archive.source[Data]` and write it to a file: the result is what
      `docker load`, `ctr images import`, `podman load` and `skopeo copy oci-archive:…`
      accept, and what `data.open[Image]()` reads back.

entry .Layer   one image layer, with its two digests: `diffId` of the raw content and `digest` of the stored blob
  detail
      A layer carries two distinct digests, and the OCI format depends on both being right.
      The `#diffId` is the SHA-256 of the *uncompressed* content, recorded in the image
      config's `rootfs.diff_ids`; the `#digest` is the SHA-256 of the blob *as stored*,
      recorded in the manifest's layer descriptor alongside its `size`. For a filesystem
      layer the two differ, because storage gzip-compresses the tar; for a blob layer they
      coincide. Both are computed lazily from the content, once.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val layer: Layer = Layer(Tarfile(Nil))
      layer.diffId == layer.digest   // false: the stored blob is gzipped
      layer.descriptor.mediaType     // media"application/vnd.oci.image.layer.v1.tar+gzip"

      val wasm: Layer = Layer.blob(hex"0061736d01000000", media"application/wasm")
      wasm.diffId == wasm.digest     // true: stored verbatim
      ```

      A `Layer` has no public constructor: `.apply` makes a filesystem layer from a
      `Tarfile`, and `.blob` makes a single-blob layer of any media type. The content is
      held by name until first use, and memoized then.
  refer embarcadero.Image
  refer embarcadero.Descriptor
  entry .apply   a filesystem layer from a `Tarfile`, stored gzip-compressed
      `Layer(tar)` reads the tar's bytes as the raw content, compresses them with gzip for
      the stored blob, and types the descriptor `application/vnd.oci.image.layer.v1.tar+gzip`.
      Nothing is read until a digest or the blob is first asked for.
  entry .blob   a layer whose content is one opaque blob, stored verbatim under its own media type
      `Layer.blob(data, mediaType)` is the form a Wasm OCI Artifact uses for its
      `application/wasm` component: no compression, so `#diffId` and `#digest` are the same
      value and the descriptor's `size` is the data's length.
  entry #raw   the uncompressed content: the tar bytes, or the blob itself
  entry #blob   the content as stored: the gzipped tar, or the blob itself
  entry #diffId   `sha256:<hex>` of `#raw`, as the image config's `rootfs.diff_ids` records it
  entry #digest   `sha256:<hex>` of `#blob`, as the manifest's layer descriptor records it
  entry #descriptor   the layer's `Descriptor`: media type, `#digest` and the stored blob's size

entry .Oci   the namespace for OCI document types with generic names: `Oci.Manifest`, `Oci.Config`, `Oci.Error`
  detail
      An object holding the OCI types whose natural names are too generic for the `soundness`
      umbrella: `Manifest` would clash with revolution's JAR `Manifest`, and `Config` and
      `Error` are everyone's. `.Manifest` is the image manifest document, `.Config` the union
      of the two config-blob forms, and `.Error` what reading an archive raises.
  refer embarcadero.Image
  entry .Config   the two forms an image's config blob can take: `Image.Config | WasmConfig`
      A type alias. The two are distinguished on the wire by the config descriptor's media
      type (`application/vnd.oci.image.config.v1+json` or
      `application/vnd.wasm.config.v0+json`); everything above the config blob — manifest,
      index, image layout — is shared. `Image#config` and `Image.Handle#config` have this
      type, and a `match` on `Image.Config` and `WasmConfig` separates them.
  entry .Manifest   an OCI image manifest: a descriptor for the config blob and the ordered layer descriptors
    detail
        The document (media type `application/vnd.oci.image.manifest.v1+json`) which ties an
        image together: `#config` is the `Descriptor` of the config blob and `#layers` the
        `Descriptor` of each layer, bottom-most first, so a reader can fetch and verify every
        blob by digest. `#schemaVersion` is always `2`. `Image.apply` and `Image.wasm` build
        one; `Image.Handle#manifest` reads one back, digest-verified. It encodes and decodes
        as JSON through jacinta.
    entry #schemaVersion   the manifest schema version: always `2`
    entry #mediaType   the manifest's own media type, `application/vnd.oci.image.manifest.v1+json`
    entry #config   the descriptor of the config blob, whose media type says which `Oci.Config` form it is
    entry #layers   the descriptors of the layers, in order
    entry #annotations   free-form `Map[Text, Text]` annotations, or `Unset`
  entry .Error   raised when an OCI archive cannot be read: a missing document, a bad digest, an unsupported form
    detail
        Error 285: _the OCI image archive could not be read because `reason`_. Raised by
        `Image.Handle`'s methods and by the openables, with a `.Reason` saying what went
        wrong. The reasons: `MissingLayout` (no `oci-layout` marker), `UnsupportedLayout
        (version)` (a layout version not beginning `1.`), `MissingIndex` (no `index.json`),
        `MissingBlob(digest)`, `DigestMismatch(expected, actual)` (a blob whose bytes do not
        hash to the digest it is addressed by), `InvalidBlob(digest, detail)` (a document
        which does not parse, or a blob whose size disagrees with its descriptor),
        `UnsupportedDigest(algorithm)` (a digest not prefixed `sha256:`), `NoManifest` (an
        index listing no manifests) and `WriteUnsupported` (an archive opened with `Write`).

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import codepages.utf8Codepage

        val archive: Data = t"not an archive".in[Data]
        safely(archive.open[Image]() { handle ?=> handle.index })   // Unset
        ```

        Under `safely` any failure is `Unset`; `capture[Oci.Error]` keeps the error so that
        its `#reason` can be matched.
    entry .Reason   why an archive could not be read, as an enum with a number per case
        The number is the error's sub-code:

        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Code
        column  Message
        column  Note
        row  `MissingLayout`  1  _the archive does not contain an oci-layout marker_
        row  `UnsupportedLayout(version)`  2  _the image layout version `version` is not supported_
        row  `MissingIndex`  3  _the archive does not contain an index.json document_
        row  `MissingBlob(digest)`  4  _the blob `digest` is not present in the archive_
        row  `DigestMismatch(expected, actual)`  5  _the blob addressed as `expected` has the digest `actual`_
        row  `InvalidBlob(digest, detail)`  6  _the blob `digest` could not be interpreted: `detail`_
        row  `UnsupportedDigest(algorithm)`  7  _the digest algorithm `algorithm` is not supported_
        row  `NoManifest`  8  _the image index lists no manifests_
        row  `WriteUnsupported`  9  _OCI image archives cannot be opened for writing_
        ```
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message, such as _the blob `digest` is not present in the archive_
    entry #reason   the `Oci.Error.Reason` which caused the failure

entry .Descriptor   an OCI content descriptor: a media type, a `sha256:…` digest and a size, pointing at a blob
  detail
      The typed, digest-addressed pointer every OCI document uses to name a blob: a
      manifest's `config` and `layers`, an index's `manifests`. `#digest` is the
      `sha256:<lowercase hex>` of the blob as stored (for a layer, compressed) and `#size`
      its byte length, so a reader can fetch the blob and check both. `Layer#descriptor`
      makes one for a layer, and `Image#configDescriptor` and `Image#manifestDescriptor` for
      the documents. The spec's `platform` member for multi-architecture indexes is not yet
      modelled. It is the OCI JSON descriptor; containerd's protobuf one is
      `Containerd.ContentDescriptor`.
  refer embarcadero.Layer
  refer embarcadero.Containerd.ContentDescriptor
  entry #mediaType   the blob's media type, such as `application/vnd.oci.image.layer.v1.tar+gzip`
  entry #digest   the `sha256:<hex>` digest of the stored blob
  entry #size   the stored blob's length in bytes
  entry #annotations   free-form `Map[Text, Text]` annotations, or `Unset`
  entry #urls   URLs the blob may be fetched from, or `Unset`

entry .Index   an OCI image index: the top-level `index.json` listing one or more manifest descriptors
  detail
      The document (media type `application/vnd.oci.image.index.v1+json`) at the root of an
      image layout, whose `#manifests` are the `Descriptor`s of the manifests the archive
      holds. `Image.apply` and `Image.wasm` write a single-entry index; `Image.Handle#index`
      reads one back, and `Image.Handle#manifest` takes its first entry by default. It
      encodes and decodes as JSON through jacinta.
  entry #schemaVersion   the index schema version: always `2`
  entry #mediaType   the index's own media type, `application/vnd.oci.image.index.v1+json`
  entry #manifests   the descriptors of the manifests, in order
  entry #annotations   free-form `Map[Text, Text]` annotations, or `Unset`

entry .History   one entry of an image config's `history`: how a layer, or an empty change, was produced
  detail
      A record in `Image.Config#history`, one per layer or per empty change, in order. Every
      field is optional and omitted from the JSON when unset; the member names are the wire
      names, so `created_by` and `empty_layer` keep their underscores.
  entry #created   when the change was made, as RFC 3339 text, or `Unset`
  entry #created_by   the command which produced the layer, or `Unset`
  entry #author   who made the change, or `Unset`
  entry #comment   a free-form note, or `Unset`
  entry #empty_layer   `true` when the entry produced no filesystem layer, or `Unset`

entry .RootFs   the `rootfs` of an image config: the ordered `diff_id`s of the uncompressed layers
  detail
      `RootFs(type, diff_ids)`, where `type` is always `layers` and `diff_ids` lists each
      layer's `Layer#diffId` bottom-most first. `Image.apply` builds it from the layers; a
      reader checks an unpacked layer's uncompressed tar against the matching entry. The
      member names are the wire names.
  entry #type   the rootfs type: always `layers`
  entry #diff_ids   the layers' `diffId`s, in order

entry .ContainerConfig   the execution parameters of an image: user, environment, entrypoint, command, working directory, labels
  detail
      The `config` member of an `Image.Config`: what a runtime does with the image's
      filesystem. The member names are capitalised to match the wire format inherited from
      the Docker image spec (`Cmd`, `Env`, `WorkingDir`), and every field is optional and
      omitted from the JSON when unset.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val config: ContainerConfig =
        ContainerConfig
          ( Env        = List(t"PATH=/usr/bin:/bin"),
            Entrypoint = List(t"/usr/bin/server"),
            Cmd        = List(t"--port", t"8080"),
            WorkingDir = t"/app",
            Labels     = Map(t"org.opencontainers.image.title" -> t"server") )
      ```
  refer embarcadero.Image
  entry #User   the user (and optionally group) to run as, or `Unset`
  entry #Env   environment variables as `NAME=value` entries, or `Unset`
  entry #Entrypoint   the executable and its fixed arguments, or `Unset`
  entry #Cmd   the default arguments, appended to `Entrypoint` or run alone, or `Unset`
  entry #WorkingDir   the working directory the process starts in, or `Unset`
  entry #Labels   free-form `Map[Text, Text]` labels, or `Unset`
  entry #StopSignal   the signal which stops the container, such as `SIGTERM`, or `Unset`

entry .WasmComponent   the `component` member of a Wasm artifact config: the interfaces it exports and imports, and its target world
  detail
      What a Wasm OCI Artifact says about the Component Model contract its component was
      built to: `#exports`, the interfaces it offers (`wasi:http/incoming-handler@0.2.0`),
      `#imports`, the capabilities it needs (`wasi:io/streams@0.2.0`), and `#target`, the
      standard world it fits (`wasi:http/proxy@0.2.0`), if any. The Wasm OCI Artifact layout
      requires this member for `wasip2` artifacts, and it is what lets a host decide, before
      fetching the component, whether it can satisfy the workload. `Image.wasm` builds it
      from its `exports`, `imports` and `target` parameters, usually read from the WIT world
      the component was linked against.
  refer embarcadero.WasmConfig
  entry #exports   the interfaces the component exports, as `namespace:package/interface@version` names
  entry #imports   the interfaces the component imports
  entry #target   the standard world the component targets, or `Unset`

entry .WasmConfig   a Wasm artifact config document: the WASI generation, the component blob's digest and its interfaces
  detail
      The config blob of a Wasm OCI Artifact (media type
      `application/vnd.wasm.config.v0+json`). It stands where an `Image.Config` would, but
      describes a component rather than a filesystem: there is no `rootfs`, because there
      are no layers to stack; `#layerDigests` names the single `application/wasm` blob
      directly, and `#component` states the interfaces it needs. `#architecture` is `wasm`
      and `#os` names the WASI generation (`wasip1`, `wasip2` or `wasip3`): a runtime
      dispatches on those two fields to recognise a Wasm workload at all. `Image.wasm` builds
      one, `Image#wasmConfig` and `Image.Handle#wasmConfig` give it back, and it is one arm
      of `Oci.Config`. The JSON uses the spec's camelCase `layerDigests` key, and omits
      `created` and `author` when unset.
  refer embarcadero.Image
  refer embarcadero.WasmComponent
  entry #created   when the artifact was built, as RFC 3339 text, or `Unset`
  entry #author   who built the artifact, or `Unset`
  entry #architecture   always `wasm`
  entry #os   the WASI generation: `wasip1`, `wasip2` (the default) or `wasip3`
  entry #layerDigests   the digests of the artifact's layers: the one `application/wasm` blob
  entry #component   the `WasmComponent` naming the exports, imports and target world, or `Unset`

entry .ImageOpenable   the `Openable` which opens a filesystem path as an OCI archive, read-only
  detail
      In the JVM source set, since it reads the tar from disk through bitumen's
      `TarOpenable`. `ImageOpenable[path]` opens any `path` which is `Abstractable across
      Paths to Text` (`Self`) as an `Image` (`Form`), with no flags (`Operand = Nothing`),
      giving an `Image.Handle` (`Result`) for the block. It needs a `Tactic[Oci.Error]`, a
      `Tactic[Tar.Error]` and a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`, and refuses a mode including
      `Write` with `Oci.Error` (`WriteUnsupported`). The given `imagePathOpenable` provides
      it, so a program writes `path.open[Image]()` rather than naming the class.
  refer embarcadero.imagePathOpenable
  entry #Self   the path type opened
  entry #Form   `Image`: the form it is opened as
  entry #Operand   `Nothing`: there are no open flags
  entry #Result   `Image.Handle`: what the block receives
  entry #open   opens the path as a tar, builds an `Image.Handle` over its entries and runs the block

entry .imagePathOpenable   the given behind `path.open[Image]()` on the JVM: opens an archive file as an image
    A top-level given in the JVM source set, exported through `soundness.*` so that it is in
    scope with the umbrella import. It provides an `ImageOpenable[path]` for any path type
    `Abstractable across Paths to Text`, capturing the `Tactic[Oci.Error]`,
    `Tactic[Tar.Error]` and `Tactic[Truncation.Error]` it needs. The handle lives for the
    block alone.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val file: Path on Linux = % / "tmp" / "image.tar"

    file.open[Image](): handle ?=>
      handle.manifest.layers.map(_.digest)
    ```

entry .Containerd   a connected containerd client: one gRPC call per method, bound to a namespace
  detail
      `Containerd(endpoint, namespace)` connects to a containerd daemon over an
      `Http2.Endpoint` — normally a Unix socket such as `/run/containerd/containerd.sock`
      carrying cleartext HTTP/2 — and binds every call to one containerd namespace, sent as
      the mandatory `containerd-namespace` header. The connection runs background daemons,
      so the client must be made inside a `supervise` scope, and it retains its `#channel`,
      which holds the ambient `Monitor`: a `Containerd` is itself a capability, written
      `Containerd^` where its type is stated.

      Each method is one unary gRPC call on the shared channel, exchanging the typed message
      records of the `containerd` component: `#version`; `#containers`, `#container`,
      `#createContainer` and `#deleteContainer` on the containers service; `#namespaces`,
      `#createNamespace` and `#deleteNamespace`; `#images`, `#image` and `#deleteImage`;
      and, for the tasks which run inside containers, `#createTask`, `#startTask`,
      `#killTask`, `#waitTask`, `#deleteTask`, `#task` and `#tasks`. Every call needs a
      `Monitor` and tactics for `Grpc.Error` (a non-zero gRPC status from the daemon),
      `Http2.Error`, `Async.Error` and `Protobuf.Error`, and each call which changes the
      daemon logs a `Containerd.Event` at `info`, so a `Containerd.Event is Loggable` must be in
      scope.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import logging.silentLogging
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import threads.virtualThreads

      supervise:
        val socket = Uds(t"/run/containerd/containerd.sock")
        val client = Containerd(Http2.Endpoint(socket, t"localhost"), t"default")

        client.version().version
        client.containers().map(container => (container.id, container.image))
        client.images().map(_.name)

        val rootfs = List(Containerd.Mount(t"overlay", t"overlay", t"/", List(t"lowerdir=/a")))
        client.createContainer(Containerd.Container(t"web", image = t"docker.io/library/alpine:latest"))
        client.createTask(t"web", rootfs)
        val pid = client.startTask(t"web")
        client.waitTask(t"web").exitStatus
        client.deleteTask(t"web")
        client.deleteContainer(t"web")
      ```

      A container is a definition; a *task* is a running instance of one, created from the
      container with the `Mount`s of its root filesystem, started, signalled, awaited and
      deleted in that order (containerd refuses to delete a task it has not reaped). The
      `Workload` openable does that sequence for a scope, with teardown on every exit path.
  refer embarcadero.Workload
  refer embarcadero.Containerd.Container
  refer embarcadero.Containerd.Event
  entry .apply   connects to a containerd endpoint, binding every call to a namespace
      `Containerd(endpoint, namespace)` takes an `Http2.Endpoint` over any `Connectable`
      and `Showable` endpoint type and the namespace name, opens a `Grpc.Channel` whose
      default metadata carries `containerd-namespace: <namespace>`, and returns the client.
      It needs a `Monitor` and a `Probate` (from `supervise`, with a `probates` given) and
      tactics for `Async.Error` and `Socket.Error`, the latter raised if the socket cannot be
      connected. The result captures the monitor.
  entry #channel   the `Grpc.Channel` every call is made on, carrying the namespace in its default metadata
  entry #version   the daemon's version and build revision, as a `VersionResponse`
      The `containerd.services.version.v1.Version` call, with an `Empty` request.
  entry #containers   the containers in the bound namespace, optionally narrowed by containerd filters
      `client.containers()` or `client.containers(filters)`, the containers service's `List`
      call. The filters are containerd filter expressions, such as `labels."tier"==db`; an
      empty list returns everything.
  entry #createContainer   registers a container, returning it as the daemon stored it
      The containers service's `Create` call. The `Container`'s `spec` carries the OCI
      runtime spec as an `AnyMessage`, passed through verbatim, and its `runtime` names the
      runtime plugin (`io.containerd.runc.v2`). Logs `Containerd.Event.ContainerCreated`.
  entry #container   a single container by id, as a `Container`
      The containers service's `Get` call; a container the daemon does not have is a
      `Grpc.Error`.
  entry #deleteContainer   removes a container by id
      The containers service's `Delete` call. Logs `Containerd.Event.ContainerDeleted`. A
      container with a live task cannot be deleted: delete the task first.
  entry #namespaces   the namespaces on the daemon, optionally filtered
      `client.namespaces()` or `client.namespaces(filter)`, the namespaces service's `List`
      call, which is not itself scoped by the bound namespace.
  entry #createNamespace   creates a namespace, returning it as stored
      The namespaces service's `Create` call, with a `Namespace` of a name and labels.
  entry #deleteNamespace   removes a namespace by name
      The namespaces service's `Delete` call.
  entry #images   the images in the bound namespace, optionally filtered, as `ImageRecord`s
      `client.images()` or `client.images(filters)`, the images service's `List` call. An
      `ImageRecord` is the daemon's metadata — name, labels and a `ContentDescriptor` of
      the manifest — not the image content; an image is loaded from an `Image#archive`
      with `ctr images import`, since the content store is not part of this client.
  entry #image   a single image by reference, as an `ImageRecord`
      The images service's `Get` call, by a reference such as
      `docker.io/library/alpine:latest`.
  entry #deleteImage   removes an image by reference, optionally waiting for its content to be collected
      `client.deleteImage(name)` or `client.deleteImage(name, sync = true)`, the images
      service's `Delete` call; `sync` makes the call return only after garbage collection.
      Logs `Containerd.Event.ImageDeleted`.
  entry #createTask   creates a task for a container, with its root filesystem mounts, returning the task's pid
      `client.createTask(containerId, rootfs, options)`, the tasks service's `Create` call.
      The `rootfs` is the `Mount`s of the root filesystem (an overlay of an unpacked
      snapshot, typically) and `options` an `AnyMessage` of runtime options, empty by
      default. The result is a `CreateTaskResponse` of the container id and the host pid of
      the new, not yet started, task. Logs `Containerd.Event.TaskCreated`.
  entry #startTask   starts a created task, returning its host pid
      `client.startTask(containerId)` or `client.startTask(containerId, execId)` for an
      exec process, the tasks service's `Start` call. Logs `Containerd.Event.TaskStarted`.
  entry #killTask   sends a signal to a task, or to every process in its container
      `client.killTask(containerId, signal, execId, all)`, the tasks service's `Kill` call,
      with the signal as a number (`15` for `SIGTERM`, `9` for `SIGKILL`); `all` targets
      every process in the container. Logs `Containerd.Event.TaskKilled`.
  entry #waitTask   waits for a task to exit, returning its exit status and time
      `client.waitTask(containerId)` or `client.waitTask(containerId, execId)`, the tasks
      service's `Wait` call, which blocks until the process has exited and returns a
      `WaitResponse`. A started task must be waited on before it can be deleted.
  entry #deleteTask   deletes a task, returning its final exit status
      `client.deleteTask(containerId)` or `client.deleteTask(containerId, execId)`, the
      tasks service's `Delete` call, returning a `DeleteTaskResponse`. Logs
      `Containerd.Event.TaskDeleted`.
  entry #task   the state of a single task, as a `Workload`
      `client.task(containerId)` or `client.task(containerId, execId)`, the tasks service's
      `Get` call; `Workload#state` is the `ProcessStatus`.
  entry #tasks   every task known to the daemon, optionally filtered
      `client.tasks()` or `client.tasks(filter)`, the tasks service's `List` call.

  entry .ProcessStatus   the status of a task's process: `Unknown`, `Created`, `Running`, `Stopped`, `Paused` or `Pausing`
    detail
        containerd's process status, a protobuf enum carried in a `Workload` as its numeric
        `#code`: `Unknown` (0), `Created` (1), `Running` (2), `Stopped` (3), `Paused` (4) and
        `Pausing` (5). `Workload#state` and `Workload.Handle`'s `state()` decode a code to
        one of these, and `Workload(…, status = ProcessStatus.Running.code)` goes the other
        way.
    refer embarcadero.Workload
    entry .of   the status for a code, or `Unset` for a number the enum does not know
    entry #code   the numeric value containerd sends on the wire

  entry .Event   what the containerd client logs as it changes the daemon
    detail
        Every change `Containerd` makes to the daemon is logged at `info`: `ContainerCreated(id)`,
        `ContainerDeleted(id)`, `ImageDeleted(name)`, `TaskCreated(container)`,
        `TaskStarted(container, pid)`, `TaskKilled(container, signal)` and
        `TaskDeleted(container)`. All but `ImageDeleted` are tagged `Log.Process`;
        `ImageDeleted` is tagged `Log.Resource`. Reads (`containers`, `version`, `task`, …) log
        nothing. A `Containerd.Event is Loggable` must be in scope for every client method;
        `logging.silentLogging` discards them.
    entry .communicable   renders each event as a message, such as _started the task for container `web` as PID `4321`_

  entry .AnyMessage   the protobuf `google.protobuf.Any`: a serialized message with its type URL, passed through verbatim
    detail
        containerd carries opaque payloads — a container's OCI runtime spec, a runtime's
        options, a task's options — as `Any` messages, which this client neither builds nor
        interprets: `#typeUrl` names the message type and `#value` holds its serialized bytes
        as an `AnyMessage.Payload`. Named `AnyMessage` to keep clear of `scala.Any`. An empty
        `AnyMessage()` is the default wherever a message takes one.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import codepages.utf8Codepage

        val spec = Containerd.AnyMessage(t"types.containerd.io/opencontainers/runtime-spec/1/Spec",
            Containerd.AnyMessage.Payload(t"{}".in[Data]))

        spec.value.data.length   // 2
        ```
    entry .Payload   the serialized bytes of an `AnyMessage`, as an opaque, immutable byte array
      detail
          An opaque type over a frozen byte array rather than a bare `Data` field, so that
          protobuf derivation can see it as a plain, capture-free value. `Payload(data)` wraps
          a `Data`, `.empty` is the zero-length payload, and the `data` extension on
          `AnyMessage` reads it back. It encodes on the wire as a length-delimited field.
      entry .empty   the zero-length payload, the default `value` of an `AnyMessage`
      entry .apply   wraps a `Data` as a payload
      entry .encodable   encodes a payload as a length-delimited protobuf field
      entry .decodable   decodes a length-delimited protobuf field as a payload
    entry .data   `payload.data`: the payload's bytes as `Data`
    entry #typeUrl   the type URL naming the serialized message's type
    entry #value   the serialized message, as an `AnyMessage.Payload`

  entry .Timestamp   the protobuf `google.protobuf.Timestamp`: seconds and nanoseconds since the Unix epoch
    detail
        The timestamp containerd's records carry (`Container#createdAt`, `Workload#exitedAt`,
        …): whole `#seconds` and a `#nanos` fraction. It is **not** exported to the
        `soundness` umbrella, since aviation's `Timestamp` keeps that name: write
        `embarcadero.Timestamp`. It converts to and from any instant type through
        anticipation's generic time abstraction, so an aviation `Instant` works with no
        dependency between the two libraries, at millisecond precision: the generic
        representation is epoch milliseconds, so protobuf's sub-millisecond nanoseconds are
        not carried.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import abstractables.epochMillisecondsAbstractable
        import chronometries.unixChronometry

        val moment: Instant = Instant(1_700_000_001_000L)
        val stamp: embarcadero.Timestamp = embarcadero.Timestamp.of(moment)
        stamp.seconds                        // 1700000001L
        stamp.instant[Instant over Unix]     // moment
        ```
    entry .of   a timestamp from any instant type `Abstractable across Instants to Long`, at millisecond precision
    entry #seconds   whole seconds since the Unix epoch
    entry #nanos   the nanosecond fraction, `0` to `999999999`; a multiple of a million when made by `.of`
    entry #instant   the timestamp as any instant type `Instantiable across Instants from Long`, at millisecond precision

  entry .Container   a containerd container record: id, labels, image, runtime, OCI spec, snapshot and timestamps
    detail
        The metadata record of the containers service, with its protobuf field numbers
        preserved so that it round-trips with the daemon; fields the client does not model
        (the `extensions` map) are skipped on decode. Only `#id` is required: `Container(t"web",
        image = t"docker.io/library/alpine:latest")` is a complete definition to register with
        `Containerd#createContainer` or open as a `Workload`. A container is a definition,
        not a process; the task made for it is what runs.
    refer embarcadero.Containerd
    refer embarcadero.Workload
    entry #id   the container's id, unique in its namespace
    entry #labels   free-form `Map[Text, Text]` labels; containerd filters can select on them
    entry #image   the reference of the image the container was made from
    entry #runtime   the `Runtime` binding: the runtime plugin's name and options
    entry #spec   the OCI runtime spec, as an opaque `AnyMessage`
    entry #snapshotter   the snapshotter which holds the container's root filesystem
    entry #snapshotKey   the key of the container's snapshot
    entry #createdAt   when the daemon stored the record, as a `Timestamp`
    entry #updatedAt   when the record last changed, as a `Timestamp`
    entry #sandbox   the sandbox the container belongs to, if any

  entry .ContentDescriptor   containerd's protobuf descriptor of a blob: media type, digest, size and annotations
    detail
        The `containerd.types.Descriptor` message, as an `ImageRecord#target` points at its
        manifest. Named `ContentDescriptor` to keep clear of the OCI JSON `Descriptor`, which
        has the same meaning on the other side of the library. The `platform` member is not
        modelled.
    refer embarcadero.Descriptor
    entry #mediaType   the blob's media type, as text
    entry #digest   the `sha256:<hex>` digest of the blob
    entry #size   the blob's length in bytes
    entry #annotations   free-form `Map[Text, Text]` annotations

  entry .ImageRecord   a containerd image record: a reference name pointing at content, with labels and timestamps
    detail
        The metadata record of the images service, which `Containerd#images` and
        `Containerd#image` return: `#name` is the reference (`docker.io/library/alpine:latest`)
        and `#target` the `ContentDescriptor` of the manifest in the content store. Named
        `ImageRecord` to keep clear of the OCI `Image` this library builds; the record is the
        daemon's index entry, not the content.
    refer embarcadero.Image
    entry #name   the image reference
    entry #labels   free-form `Map[Text, Text]` labels
    entry #target   the `ContentDescriptor` of the image's manifest
    entry #createdAt   when the daemon stored the record, as a `Timestamp`
    entry #updatedAt   when the record last changed, as a `Timestamp`

  entry .Mount   a mount in a task's root filesystem: a source mounted at a target with a type and options
    detail
        The `containerd.types.Mount` message, given to `Containerd#createTask` as the task's
        `rootfs` and to `container.open[Workload](…)` as its flags; `#mountType` is the proto's
        `type`. An overlay of an unpacked snapshot is the common case:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val rootfs: List[Containerd.Mount] =
          List(Containerd.Mount(t"overlay", t"overlay", t"/", List(t"lowerdir=/a", t"upperdir=/b", t"workdir=/c")))
        ```
    entry #mountType   the filesystem type, such as `overlay` or `bind`
    entry #source   what is mounted
    entry #target   where it is mounted, within the task's root
    entry #options   the mount options, as `key=value` entries

  entry .Namespace   a containerd namespace: an isolation scope for containers, images and tasks, with labels
    detail
        The record of the namespaces service: a `#name` (`default`, `k8s.io`, `moby`) and
        free-form `#labels`. A `Containerd` client is bound to one namespace at connection;
        `Containerd#namespaces`, `#createNamespace` and `#deleteNamespace` manage them.
    entry #name   the namespace's name
    entry #labels   free-form `Map[Text, Text]` labels

  entry .Runtime   a container's runtime binding: the runtime plugin's name and its opaque options
    detail
        The `Container.Runtime` message: `#name` is the plugin (`io.containerd.runc.v2` for a
        filesystem image, `io.containerd.wasmtime.v1` for a Wasm artifact under a wasmtime
        shim) and `#options` an `AnyMessage` of its options. The name is exported to the
        `soundness` umbrella as `Runtime`, so no other library may export one.
    entry #name   the runtime plugin's name
    entry #options   the plugin's options, as an opaque `AnyMessage`

  entry .Empty   the protobuf `google.protobuf.Empty`: a message with no fields
      The request of parameterless calls such as `Containerd#version`, and the reply of the
      delete calls. `Empty()` is its only value.

  entry .VersionResponse   the reply of containerd's `Version` call: the daemon's version and git revision
    detail
        What `Containerd#version` returns: `#version` is the semantic version (`1.7.0`) and
        `#revision` the git revision the daemon was built from.
    entry #version   the daemon's semantic version
    entry #revision   the git revision it was built from

  entry .CreateContainerRequest   the request of the containers service's `Create`: the container to register
    detail
        The `…Request` and `…Response` types are the containerd gRPC protocol mirrored field by field, each
        a case class with protobuf field numbers which `Containerd`'s methods build and
        decode, exported so that a program can speak to the daemon directly through a
        `Grpc.Channel` or encode them with `in[Protobuf]`. For the containers service:
        `CreateContainerRequest(container)` and `CreateContainerResponse(container)`,
        `GetContainerRequest(id)` and `GetContainerResponse(container)`,
        `ListContainersRequest(filters)` and `ListContainersResponse(containers)`, and
        `DeleteContainerRequest(id)`, whose reply is `Empty`.
    entry #container   the `Container` to register

  entry .CreateContainerResponse   the reply of the containers service's `Create`: the container as stored
    entry #container   the stored `Container`

  entry .GetContainerRequest   the request of the containers service's `Get`: a container id
    entry #id   the id of the container to fetch

  entry .GetContainerResponse   the reply of the containers service's `Get`: the container
    entry #container   the requested `Container`

  entry .ListContainersRequest   the request of the containers service's `List`: optional filter expressions
    entry #filters   containerd filter expressions, such as `labels."tier"==db`; empty for everything

  entry .ListContainersResponse   the reply of the containers service's `List`: the matching containers
    entry #containers   the matching `Container`s, in containerd's order

  entry .DeleteContainerRequest   the request of the containers service's `Delete`: a container id; the reply is `Empty`
    entry #id   the id of the container to remove

  entry .CreateNamespaceRequest   the request of the namespaces service's `Create`: the namespace to create
    detail
        The namespaces service's messages: `CreateNamespaceRequest(namespace)` and
        `CreateNamespaceResponse(namespace)`, `ListNamespacesRequest(filter)` and
        `ListNamespacesResponse(namespaces)`, and `DeleteNamespaceRequest(name)`, whose reply
        is `Empty`.
    entry #namespace   the `Namespace` to create

  entry .CreateNamespaceResponse   the reply of the namespaces service's `Create`: the namespace as stored
    entry #namespace   the stored `Namespace`

  entry .ListNamespacesRequest   the request of the namespaces service's `List`: an optional filter expression
    entry #filter   a containerd filter expression; empty for everything

  entry .ListNamespacesResponse   the reply of the namespaces service's `List`: the matching namespaces
    entry #namespaces   the matching `Namespace`s

  entry .DeleteNamespaceRequest   the request of the namespaces service's `Delete`: a namespace name; the reply is `Empty`
    entry #name   the name of the namespace to remove

  entry .GetImageRequest   the request of the images service's `Get`: an image reference
    detail
        The images service's messages: `GetImageRequest(name)` and `GetImageResponse(image)`,
        `ListImagesRequest(filters)` and `ListImagesResponse(images)`, and
        `DeleteImageRequest(name, sync)`, whose reply is `Empty`.
    entry #name   the reference of the image to fetch

  entry .GetImageResponse   the reply of the images service's `Get`: the image record
    entry #image   the requested `ImageRecord`

  entry .ListImagesRequest   the request of the images service's `List`: optional filter expressions
    entry #filters   containerd filter expressions; empty for everything

  entry .ListImagesResponse   the reply of the images service's `List`: the matching image records
    entry #images   the matching `ImageRecord`s

  entry .DeleteImageRequest   the request of the images service's `Delete`: an image reference and whether to wait; the reply is `Empty`
    entry #name   the reference of the image to remove
    entry #sync   whether to return only after the content has been garbage-collected

  entry .CreateTaskRequest   the request of the tasks service's `Create`: the container, its rootfs mounts, stdio and options
    detail
        The tasks service's messages: `CreateTaskRequest` and `CreateTaskResponse(containerId,
        pid)`, `StartRequest(containerId, execId)` and `StartResponse(pid)`,
        `KillRequest(containerId, execId, signal, all)` with an `Empty` reply,
        `WaitRequest(containerId, execId)` and `WaitResponse(exitStatus, exitedAt)`,
        `DeleteTaskRequest(containerId, execId)` and `DeleteTaskResponse(id, pid, exitStatus,
        exitedAt)`, `GetTaskRequest(containerId, execId)` and `GetTaskResponse(process)`, and
        `ListTasksRequest(filter)` and `ListTasksResponse(tasks)`. An empty `execId` means the
        container's main task; a non-empty one names an exec process within it.
    entry #containerId   the container to create the task for
    entry #rootfs   the `Mount`s of the task's root filesystem
    entry #stdin   the path the task's standard input is read from; empty for none
    entry #stdout   the path the task's standard output is written to; empty for none
    entry #stderr   the path the task's standard error is written to; empty for none
    entry #terminal   whether the task gets a pseudo-terminal
    entry #options   runtime options, as an opaque `AnyMessage`

  entry .CreateTaskResponse   the reply of the tasks service's `Create`: the container id and the new task's host pid
    entry #containerId   the container the task was created for
    entry #pid   the host pid of the created task

  entry .StartRequest   the request of the tasks service's `Start`: the task, or exec process, to start
    entry #containerId   the container whose task to start
    entry #execId   the exec process to start; empty for the main task

  entry .StartResponse   the reply of the tasks service's `Start`: the started process's host pid
    entry #pid   the host pid of the started process

  entry .KillRequest   the request of the tasks service's `Kill`: a signal for a task, an exec process or every process; the reply is `Empty`
    entry #containerId   the container whose task to signal
    entry #execId   the exec process to signal; empty for the main task
    entry #signal   the signal number, such as `15` for `SIGTERM`
    entry #all   whether to signal every process in the container

  entry .WaitRequest   the request of the tasks service's `Wait`: the task, or exec process, to wait for
    entry #containerId   the container whose task to wait for
    entry #execId   the exec process to wait for; empty for the main task

  entry .WaitResponse   the reply of the tasks service's `Wait`: the exit status and the time of exit
    entry #exitStatus   the process's exit status
    entry #exitedAt   when it exited, as a `Timestamp`

  entry .DeleteTaskRequest   the request of the tasks service's `Delete`: the task, or exec process, to remove
    entry #containerId   the container whose task to delete
    entry #execId   the exec process to delete; empty for the main task

  entry .DeleteTaskResponse   the reply of the tasks service's `Delete`: the removed process's id, pid, exit status and time
    entry #id   the exec id of the removed process
    entry #pid   its host pid
    entry #exitStatus   the exit status it stopped with
    entry #exitedAt   when it exited, as a `Timestamp`

  entry .GetTaskRequest   the request of the tasks service's `Get`: the task, or exec process, to look up
    entry #containerId   the container whose task to fetch
    entry #execId   the exec process to fetch; empty for the main task

  entry .GetTaskResponse   the reply of the tasks service's `Get`: the process state
    entry #process   the requested `Workload`

  entry .ListTasksRequest   the request of the tasks service's `List`: an optional filter expression
    entry #filter   a containerd filter expression; empty for everything

  entry .ListTasksResponse   the reply of the tasks service's `List`: the matching task processes
    entry #tasks   the matching `Workload`s

entry .Workload   a task's process state, and the openable which scopes a container's whole lifecycle to a block
  detail
      `Workload` is two things. As a **record**, it is a task's process state as containerd
      reports it (`containerd.v1.types.Process`, in part): the `#containerId` and exec `#id`
      it belongs to, its host `#pid`, the raw `#status` code (decoded by `#state` to a
      `Containerd.ProcessStatus`), and the `#exitStatus` and `#exitedAt` once it has stopped. It is
      what `Containerd#task` and `Containerd#tasks` return.

      As a **form**, it is what a `Containerd.Container` is opened as: `container.open[Workload]()`
      registers the container, creates a task for it with the `Containerd.Mount`s given as flags, runs
      the block with a `Workload.Handle`, and on every exit path — normal, exception or
      error — kills the task if it was started, reaps it, deletes it, and deletes the
      container. The lifetime of the workload *is* the scope. The mode says what the block
      may do: the default `Read` creates the container and task without starting it, an
      inspection scope; `Launch` starts the task and confers `await`; `Signalling` confers
      `kill`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import logging.silentLogging
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import threads.virtualThreads

      supervise:
        val socket = Uds(t"/run/containerd/containerd.sock")
        given containerd: (Containerd^) = Containerd(Http2.Endpoint(socket, t"localhost"), t"default")

        val spec = Containerd.Container(t"web", image = t"docker.io/library/alpine:latest")
        val rootfs = Containerd.Mount(t"overlay", t"overlay", t"/", List(t"lowerdir=/a"))

        val exit: Int = spec.open[Workload](Read & Launch & Signalling, rootfs): workload ?=>
          workload.pid
          workload.state()
          workload.await().exitStatus
      ```

      The `Containerd^` must be a given in scope, since `.openable` resolves through it, and
      the `Monitor` comes from `supervise`. Teardown is best-effort and never masks the
      block's own result or error: a block which throws still sees its task killed and
      deleted, and the exception escapes. A failed `createContainer` makes no further
      calls.
  refer embarcadero.Containerd
  refer embarcadero.Launch
  refer embarcadero.Signalling
  refer embarcadero.Containerd.ProcessStatus
  entry .openable   the given behind `container.open[Workload](…)`: a `Workload.Openable` over the `Containerd^` in scope
      A given in the companion, so it resolves with no import. It needs a `Containerd^` given
      and a `Monitor^`, and tactics for `Grpc.Error`, `Http2.Error`, `Async.Error` and
      `Protobuf.Error`, all of which it captures.
  entry .Grant   the grants particular to workloads: `Launch` (the task is started) and `Signal` (it may be signalled)
    detail
        Two traits in aperture's open `Grant` hierarchy, selected by the modes `Launch` and
        `Signalling` and refining the `Workload.Handle` a block receives, so that `await` is
        available only on a started task and `kill` only when signalling was asked for.
    entry .Launch   the grant that the task is started on open, conferring `await`
    entry .Signal   the grant conferring `kill`
        The grant keeps the name `Signal`; its mode is `Signalling`, since profanity's
        interrupt holds `Signal` in the `soundness` umbrella.
  entry .Handle   the scoped handle to a running or created task: its pid, state, exit and signals
    detail
        What the block of `container.open[Workload](…)` receives: an exclusive capability
        holding the `#containerId` and `#pid` of the task made for the scope. Its operations
        are extensions in the companion, gated by the grants the mode conferred, rather than
        methods: `.state` on any handle, `.await` on a `Launch`ed one, `.kill` on a
        `Signalling` one. Each is a call to the daemon and needs the same `Monitor` and
        tactics as `Containerd`'s methods. The handle cannot escape its block.
    entry .state   the task's current `ProcessStatus`, by asking the daemon
        `workload.state()`, available on every handle: `Containerd#task` for the handle's
        container, decoded to `Created`, `Running`, `Stopped`, `Paused`, `Pausing` or
        `Unknown`.
    entry .await   waits for a launched task to exit, returning its `WaitResponse`
        `workload.await()`, available when the mode included `Launch`. Named `await` rather
        than `wait`, which `Object#wait` holds.
    entry .kill   sends a signal to the task, or to every process in its container
        `workload.kill(signal)` or `workload.kill(signal, all = true)`, available when the
        mode included `Signalling`.
    entry #containerId   the id of the container the scope registered
    entry #pid   the task's host pid: of the started process under `Launch`, else of the created task
  entry .Openable   the `Openable` which opens a `Container` as a `Workload`, with `Mount`s as its flags
    detail
        The class `Workload.openable` provides, named so that its result type is stated
        exactly. It opens a `Containerd.Container` (`Self`) as a `Workload` (`Form`), taking the
        `Containerd.Mount`s of the root filesystem as flags (`Operand`), and gives a `Workload.Handle`
        (`Result`). It is built over a `Containerd^` and a `Monitor^` and the four tactics a
        containerd call needs.
    entry #Self   `Container`: what is opened
    entry #Form   `Workload`: the form it is opened as
    entry #Operand   `Mount`: the flags are the root filesystem's mounts
    entry #Result   `Workload.Handle`: what the block receives
    entry #open   creates the container and its task, starts it under `Launch`, runs the block, and tears everything down
        The sequence is `createContainer`, `createTask` with the flags as `rootfs`,
        `startTask` if the mode includes `Launch`, the block, then — in a `finally` —
        `killTask(9, all = true)` and `waitTask` if it was started, `deleteTask`, and
        `deleteContainer`. Each teardown step is wrapped in `safely`, so a failure there
        cannot mask the block's result.
  entry #containerId   the container the task belongs to
  entry #id   the exec id within the container; empty for the container's main task
  entry #pid   the task's host pid
  entry #status   the raw containerd status code; `#state` decodes it
  entry #exitStatus   the exit status, once the task has stopped
  entry #exitedAt   when the task exited, as a `Timestamp`
  entry #state   the `#status` code as a `ProcessStatus`, `Unknown` for a code the enum does not know

entry .Launch   the open mode which starts the task: `container.open[Workload](Read & Launch)`
    A `Mode granting Workload.Grant.Launch`, composed with aperture's `Read` and with
    `Signalling` by `&`. With it, `Workload.Openable` calls `startTask` after creating the
    task, the handle's `pid` is the started process's, `await` becomes available, and
    teardown kills and reaps the task before deleting it. Without it the task is created but
    never started.

entry .Signalling   the open mode which lets the block signal the task: `container.open[Workload](Read & Launch & Signalling)`
    A `Mode granting Workload.Grant.Signal`, composed with `Read` and `Launch` by `&`. With
    it, `kill` is available on the `Workload.Handle`. The name is `Signalling`, not `Signal`,
    because profanity's interrupt holds `Signal` in the `soundness` umbrella; the grant
    itself is still `Workload.Grant.Signal`.
